    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The RB1 tumor suppressor gene (also known as RB) is frequently silenced, deleted, or mutated in a wide range of human tumors. When the RB gene is not inactivated, the RB protein is typically functionally inactivated by hyperphosphorylation. These observations have led to multiple loss-of-function experiments in cellular and animal models, which have demonstrated that loss of RB is a strong promoter of cancer development. In contrast, very few studies have tested the hypothesis that continuous RB loss is required to maintain cancer phenotypes and that RB reactivation will suppress cancer, even though these questions are highly clinically relevant due to a new class of drugs inhibiting the RB kinases CDK4/6. Since these CDK4/6 inhibitors often do not stop cancer cells as well as expected, the crucial question is whether RB re-activation is in fact truly an effective anti-cancer therapy. To research this question, we have generated a novel allele of RB in mice in which RB expression can be controlled at will (doxycycline-inducible shRNA against Rb, shRb). This allele has allowed us to investigate the consequences of RB loss and RB reactivation in a flexible manner both in vivo and in vitro. Using this allele, we have found that long term addition of doxycycline to the drinking water of shRb mice is sufficient to result in the development of pituitary and thyroid tumors, as well as accelerate aging phenotypes such as weight loss, unhealthy hair, and kyphosis, all which reduced the life span of the mouse. Removal of doxycycline from the drinking water stabilizes the progression of disease phenotypes in the mouse and significantly decreases proliferative markers and expression of E2F target genes in pituitary cancer cells within 6 weeks. These results overall suggest a great potential for RB reactivation to be a therapeutic mechanism to treat cancers initiated by the loss of RB.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
